        h2 §The Rust core allocation and collections library
        text This library provides smart pointers and collections for managing heap-allocated values.
        text This library, like core, normally doesn’t need to be used directly since its contents are re-exported in the . Crates that use the  attribute however will typically not depend on , so they’d use this crate instead.
        link "std crate" ../std/index.html
        text "#![no_std]"
        text std
        h3 §Boxed values
        text The  type is a smart pointer type. There can only be one owner of a , and the owner can decide to mutate the contents, which live on the heap.
        row
          link Box boxed/index.html
          link Box boxed/index.html
        text This type can be sent among threads efficiently as the size of a Box value is the same as that of a pointer. Tree-like data structures are often built with boxes because each node often has only one owner, the parent.
        h3 §Reference counted pointers
        text The  type is a non-threadsafe reference-counted pointer type intended for sharing memory within a thread. An  pointer wraps a type, , and only allows access to , a shared reference.
        link Rc rc/index.html
        link Rc rc/index.html
        text T
        text &T
        text This type is useful when inherited mutability (such as using ) is too constraining for an application, and is often paired with the  or  types in order to allow mutation.
        link Box boxed/index.html
        link Cell ../core/cell/index.html
        link RefCell ../core/cell/index.html
        h3 §Atomically reference counted pointers
        text The  type is the threadsafe equivalent of the  type. It provides all the same functionality of , except it requires that the contained type  is shareable. Additionally,  is itself sendable while  is not.
        link Arc sync/index.html
        link Rc rc/index.html
        link Rc rc/index.html
        text T
        link Arc<T> sync/index.html
        link Rc<T> rc/index.html
        text This type allows for shared access to the contained data, and is often paired with synchronization primitives such as mutexes to allow mutation of shared resources.
        h3 §Collections
        text Implementations of the most common general purpose data structures are defined in this library. They are re-exported through the .
        link "standard collections library" ../std/collections/index.html
        h3 §Heap interfaces
        text The  module defines the low-level interface to the default global allocator. It is not compatible with the libc allocator API.
        link alloc alloc/index.html
